// THE AUTHORED PRODUCER'S ADDRESSING — slot A of the seam, everything except the union itself.
//
// Docs/Clouds/ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4 gives the programme two producers behind one seam. P, the
// procedural one, lives in Common/CloudField.glslh and answers everywhere. A, this one, answers only
// inside the bounds of an INSTANCE: a sculpted volume placed in the sky by an entity's transform, which
// is where a silhouette the procedural field cannot make comes from. This file is what an instance IS
// and how a point in the sky becomes a point in its volume; Common/CloudField.glslh is where the two
// answers meet.
//
// WHY THE ADDRESSING IS SPLIT OUT OF THE SEAM AT ALL, since it is only forty lines: the instance array
// is a storage block, a storage block cannot be compiled as C++, and Common/CloudField.glslh has to be —
// it is the file the CloudField suite drives. Declaring the block here, before the seam includes it,
// is the same arrangement CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE already has: the data arrives through a macro that the
// march defines as a fetch and a test defines as a call into the very generator that wrote the file.
//
// HOW THE DATA ARRIVES, and the caller owns every one of these:
//
//     CLOUD_AUTHORED_COUNT                      -> int, how many instances are live this frame, 0..8
//     CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLAB_COUNT                 -> int, how many BODIES the atlas holds, 0..8
//     CLOUD_AUTHORED_INSTANCE( int i )          -> CloudAuthoredInstance
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( vec3 uvw )         -> vec4, four channels of the modelling volume ATLAS
//
// A shader gets the first three for free by defining CLOUD_AUTHORED_BUFFER_BINDING before including this
// file; a test defines them itself over a C++ array. The last is a sampler and therefore always the
// caller's, exactly like CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE.
//
// Dialect rules as in Common/CloudNoise.glslh. Distances are KILOMETRES.

#ifndef CLOUD_AUTHORED_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_AUTHORED_GLSLH

// HOW MANY HERO CLOUDS ONE SKY HOLDS AT ONCE. Eight, and — unlike CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS, which is the width
// of a texel — this one is a BUDGET rather than a structural limit: every instance costs the march the
// bounds test below at every field sample, and that test runs once per light-march sample as well, so it
// is multiplied by about thirty. Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md §A2 carries the slope at one, three and eight.
//
// EIGHT AND NOT TEN, AND THE BOUND IS MEMORY RATHER THAN THE MARCH. Eight DISTINCT bodies are eight slabs
// of the atlas below, 4.00 MiB each; against decision D-9's 64 MiB and the 20.67 MiB the subsystem already
// occupies at 1280x766 that leaves 11.33 MiB of headroom, and the trace and history targets grow with the
// frame — at 1920x1080 they take 9.4 MiB more, which spends almost all of it. Ten would not fit that
// frame. Instances that SHARE a body cost no memory at all, so eight is a limit on bodies and only
// incidentally on entities.
#define CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLOTS 8

// THE VOLUME'S SHAPE, FIXED BY THE FORMAT. Every `.dcmv` is exactly this size — 128 x 64 x 128 voxels,
// two horizontal axes at 128 and the vertical one at 64, because a cloud is wider than it is tall and a
// cubic volume would spend half its bytes on air. In RGBA8 that is 4.00 MiB.
//
// FIXED RATHER THAN CARRIED IN THE FILE, and the reason is the clamp below: a coordinate has to be
// pulled in by half a texel and half a texel of WHAT has to be a compile-time number here, because this
// header is also the C++ the tests drive. Assets::kCloudModellingVolumeWidth and its two siblings are
// the same three numbers on the C++ side and Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored asserts they agree — two
// statements of one fact, so the pair is a test rather than a hope.
#define CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_WIDTH  128
#define CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_HEIGHT 64
#define CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_DEPTH  128

// THE ATLAS, AND WHY IT IS AN ATLAS. Several DIFFERENT bodies in one sky need several volumes reachable
// from one dispatch, and there are exactly two ways to do that:
//
//   * an ARRAY of `sampler3D`, indexed by the instance. This engine's reflection REFUSES it in so many
//     words — VulkanShaderReflection.cpp: "arrays of descriptors are not supported — declare separate
//     bindings" — because every layout it builds hardcodes descriptorCount = 1. Eight separate bindings
//     is the version that compiles, and it costs eight descriptors in each of TWO pipelines, eight
//     fallback images to keep the set valid, and an eight-way branch around the fetch: the index is a
//     loop variable, and a non-indexable sampler can only be selected by comparing it against eight
//     constants.
//   * ONE volume holding the bodies end to end, addressed by arithmetic. One descriptor, one fallback,
//     one fetch site, and the addressing is a single multiply-add.
//
// The second, therefore, and the arithmetic is the whole of it: bodies are stacked along the DEPTH axis,
// which is the slowest-varying one, so the atlas's bytes are the bodies' bytes concatenated in slab order
// and Assets::AssembleCloudModellingAtlas is a run of memcpy rather than a re-interleave.
//
// THE SLAB BOUNDARY DOES NOT BLEED, and it takes two guarantees rather than one. An instance's own w
// never leaves [first texel centre, last texel centre] OF ITS SLAB — see CloudAuthoredAtlasUvw — so in
// real arithmetic the trilinear filter's two depth taps are two texels of the SAME body. In FLOAT, a
// division by a slab count that is not a power of two leaves up to about 3e-5 of weight on the
// neighbour's outermost texel, and the second guarantee is what makes that nothing: the bake refuses a
// volume whose body touches its own boundary, so that texel is four zeroes.
// Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored asserts both.
#define CLOUD_MODELLING_ATLAS_MAX_SLABS CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLOTS

// The four channels of the modelling volume, in the order Engine/Assets/CloudModellingVolume.hpp writes
// them. Named here because the shader reads them by swizzle and a comment three files away is not where
// the next person looks.
//
//   x  DIMENSIONAL PROFILE  how deep inside the body, 0 at the surface and 1 in the core. The same
//                           quantity the procedural producer returns, which is what makes the union a
//                           plain `max` rather than a negotiation (deck p.85).
//   y  DETAIL TYPE          0 wispy, 1 billowy — which erosion the up-rez applies here.
//   z  DENSITY SCALE        per-voxel multiplier on how much matter is in this part of the body.
//   w  CUTOUT ENVELOPE      the body dilated by the bake's margin: 1 throughout the body and falling to
//                           0 a stated distance outside it. It is the conservative shell — it is 1
//                           wherever the profile is above 0 and extends past it, never the other way
//                           round — and the cutout is what reads it.

/**
 * One hero cloud in the sky, as the march sees it.
 *
 * TWENTY FLOATS, ALL READ. The instance is a transform, a box and four material numbers, and it is laid
 * out so those nineteen values plus the strength fill five vec4s exactly rather than leaving a slot for
 * the next parameter to be stashed in without a name or a tooltip.
 *
 * THE TRANSFORM IS THE LINEAR PART ONLY, and the translation is NOT stored, because it is already here:
 * an axis-aligned box around a rotated box is centred on the same point, so the centre of the bounds
 * below IS the centre of the instance whatever its rotation. Three floats saved and, more usefully, two
 * statements of one position reduced to one — the class of defect this programme has paid for most
 * often.
 *
 * THE FRAME IS THE FIELD'S, not the world's: x and z are world kilometres, y is the ALTITUDE ABOVE THE
 * LAYER'S BASE in kilometres, which is the vector CloudRaymarch.shader hands SampleCloudField and the
 * only frame in which the noise's period means anything (see the note at that call site). The conversion
 * happens once, on the CPU, in Graphic::PackCloudAuthoredInstance.
 */
struct CloudAuthoredInstance
{
    // Rows of the linear map from a field-space DISPLACEMENT in kilometres to the volume's local cube,
    // whose corners are -0.5 and +0.5. It is the inverse of rotation x scale x the volume's own authored
    // size, so a uniformly scaled instance has rows of length 1/sizeKm.
    vec4 Row0; // xyz = row 0, w = DetailFactor      — this cloud's multiplier on the layer's erosion depth
    vec4 Row1; // xyz = row 1, w = DensityFactor     — its multiplier on the layer's density
    vec4 Row2; // xyz = row 2, w = ExtinctionFactor  — its multiplier on the layer's extinction

    // The instance's axis-aligned bounds in field space, kilometres. THE CHEAP REJECTION IS THIS BOX AND
    // NOT THE EXACT ONE, and the ratio is the reason: six compares against twenty-one multiply-adds, run
    // at every field sample of every ray AND at every sample of every shadow ray, which is about thirty
    // times more often again. The exact test follows, but only for the points this one lets through.
    //
    // THE STRENGTH IS SIGNED AND THE CUTOUT IS ITS POSITIVE PART. A0 spent one float on each and the
    // cutout was `suppress ? strength : 0` — one number derived from the other and a flag, which is the
    // duplicated state this very struct refuses for the translation. Signed, it is one number:
    //
    //     Strength = abs( BoundsMin.w )        Cutout = max( BoundsMin.w, 0 )
    //
    // and a body wound down to nothing gives the procedural sky back either way, because both are zero
    // there whatever the sign. Graphic::PackCloudAuthoredInstance writes it; the two readers are
    // CloudAuthoredStrength and CloudAuthoredCutout below, so nothing else has to know.
    //
    // The float that frees is BoundsMax.w, and it holds the ATLAS SLAB — the depth coordinate at which
    // this instance's body begins. That, and CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLAB_COUNT, is all the addressing an atlas
    // needs.
    vec4 BoundsMin; // xyz = minimum corner, w = signed Strength (negative = does not suppress)
    vec4 BoundsMax; // xyz = maximum corner, w = AtlasSlabBaseW — where the body's slab begins
};

/// How strongly this instance asserts itself, 0..1. See the note on BoundsMin.w.
float CloudAuthoredStrength( CloudAuthoredInstance instance )
{
    return abs( instance.BoundsMin.w );
}

/// How much of the procedural field it suppresses, 0..1 — zero when the artist switched the cutout off,
/// and equal to the strength otherwise, which is what makes Strength a fade rather than a hole-punch.
float CloudAuthoredCutout( CloudAuthoredInstance instance )
{
    return max( instance.BoundsMin.w, 0.0f );
}

/// True when @p positionKm is inside the instance's axis-aligned bounds. The first and cheapest of the
/// two tests, and the one that makes an authored instance cost almost nothing in the sky around it.
bool CloudAuthoredInBounds( CloudAuthoredInstance instance, vec3 positionKm )
{
    if ( positionKm.x < instance.BoundsMin.x || positionKm.x > instance.BoundsMax.x )
        return false;
    if ( positionKm.y < instance.BoundsMin.y || positionKm.y > instance.BoundsMax.y )
        return false;
    if ( positionKm.z < instance.BoundsMin.z || positionKm.z > instance.BoundsMax.z )
        return false;
    return true;
}

/**
 * Where @p positionKm lands in the instance's volume, as a texture coordinate in [0, 1].
 *
 * A point outside the instance's own box maps outside that range, and the caller MUST reject it —
 * every sampler this engine creates is REPEAT, so a coordinate of 1.2 fetches the far side of the volume
 * and a hero cloud would grow a mirror image of its own flank beside it.
 */
vec3 CloudAuthoredLocalUvw( CloudAuthoredInstance instance, vec3 positionKm )
{
    vec3 centre = ( vec3( instance.BoundsMin.x, instance.BoundsMin.y, instance.BoundsMin.z ) +
                    vec3( instance.BoundsMax.x, instance.BoundsMax.y, instance.BoundsMax.z ) ) *
                  0.5f;

    vec3 offset = positionKm - centre;

    vec3 local = vec3( dot( vec3( instance.Row0.x, instance.Row0.y, instance.Row0.z ), offset ),
                       dot( vec3( instance.Row1.x, instance.Row1.y, instance.Row1.z ), offset ),
                       dot( vec3( instance.Row2.x, instance.Row2.y, instance.Row2.z ), offset ) );

    return local + vec3( 0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f );
}

/// True when a coordinate produced by CloudAuthoredLocalUvw is inside the volume. This is the EXACT
/// bounds of a rotated instance, where CloudAuthoredInBounds is only a box around it.
bool CloudAuthoredInVolume( vec3 uvw )
{
    if ( uvw.x < 0.0f || uvw.x > 1.0f )
        return false;
    if ( uvw.y < 0.0f || uvw.y > 1.0f )
        return false;
    if ( uvw.z < 0.0f || uvw.z > 1.0f )
        return false;
    return true;
}

/// Where slab @p slot begins, as a depth coordinate of an atlas of @p slabCount bodies. The one place the
/// atlas's geometry is written down; Graphic::CloudAuthoredAtlasSlabBaseW is the same line in C++ and
/// Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored drives both over every slot of every count rather than trusting them.
float CloudAuthoredAtlasSlabBaseW( int slot, int slabCount )
{
    return float( slot ) / float( slabCount );
}

/**
 * The coordinate the fetch actually uses: @p uvw, which is in the BODY's own cube, placed in the atlas.
 *
 * ALL THREE AXES ARE CLAMPED TO TEXEL CENTRES, and it is one statement about one hazard. Every sampler in
 * this engine is REPEAT, so a coordinate of exactly 1 wraps to the first texel and the trilinear filter
 * blends one face of the cloud with the opposite one; on the depth axis that opposite face now belongs to
 * a DIFFERENT BODY, which is worse than a mirror of itself. The clamp is what makes that unreachable: the
 * depth coordinate is pulled inside the body's own texel centres BEFORE it is folded into the slab, so the
 * filter's two depth taps are always two texels of the same cloud.
 *
 * IT REDUCES TO A0's SINGLE-VOLUME ADDRESSING EXACTLY when the atlas holds one body — base 0, divisor 1 —
 * which is why a scene with one hero cloud renders the frame it rendered before the atlas existed, byte
 * for byte. Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored asserts the reduction on the arithmetic and the six-point
 * protocol asserts it on the pixels.
 *
 * The clamps cost nothing in accuracy because the bake guarantees the outermost shell of voxels is empty —
 * Assets::GenerateCloudModellingVolume refuses a volume whose body touches its own box.
 */
vec3 CloudAuthoredAtlasUvw( vec3 uvw, float slabBaseW, int slabCount )
{
    float halfU = 0.5f / float( CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_WIDTH );
    float halfV = 0.5f / float( CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_HEIGHT );
    float halfW = 0.5f / float( CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_DEPTH );

    return vec3( clamp( uvw.x, halfU, 1.0f - halfU ), clamp( uvw.y, halfV, 1.0f - halfV ),
                 slabBaseW + clamp( uvw.z, halfW, 1.0f - halfW ) / float( slabCount ) );
}

#ifdef CLOUD_AUTHORED_BUFFER_BINDING
// The instance array as the GPU receives it. The C++ half of this layout is
// Graphic::CloudAuthoredPayload (Engine/Graphic/Clouds/CloudAuthoredPayload.hpp), where a static_assert
// pins every offset — a layout declared twice diverges the first time somebody inserts a member into one
// of them, and the failure mode is a hero cloud read from the wrong bytes rather than an error.
//
// A SECOND STORAGE BLOCK RATHER THAN MORE OF THE FIRST. The cloud parameter block is the LAYER, one copy
// of it, and it is repacked only when the artist moves a slider; this is a per-frame list whose length
// changes when an entity is added to the scene. Appending an array of instances to CloudParams.glslh
// would have made the layer's block grow by 320 bytes for the scenes that have no hero cloud at all,
// which is the one thing §4.4 of the analysis forbids.
//
// THE TWO ints FIRST AND THE ARRAY AFTER THEM. std430 aligns an array of structs to 16, so the two counts
// leave eight bytes of ALIGNMENT — not a reserved slot: there is no member there to read, and the C++ side
// pins the array's offset at 16 so the two cannot disagree about it.
//
// THE SLAB COUNT IS A PROPERTY OF THE ATLAS AND THEREFORE OF THE BUFFER, not of an instance: every
// instance in a frame reads the same atlas, and a per-instance copy of one number is eight chances for
// eight copies to disagree.
layout( std430, binding = CLOUD_AUTHORED_BUFFER_BINDING ) readonly buffer CloudAuthoredBuffer
{
    int                   u_CloudAuthoredCount;
    int                   u_CloudAuthoredSlabCount;
    CloudAuthoredInstance u_CloudAuthoredInstances[CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLOTS];
};

#define CLOUD_AUTHORED_COUNT         u_CloudAuthoredCount
#define CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLAB_COUNT    u_CloudAuthoredSlabCount
#define CLOUD_AUTHORED_INSTANCE( i ) u_CloudAuthoredInstances[i]
#endif // CLOUD_AUTHORED_BUFFER_BINDING

#endif // CLOUD_AUTHORED_GLSLH
